[2026-07-16] Redesign Phase 2 — primitive component library
Files (new): components/ui/{button,input,textarea,select,field,card,empty-state,alert}.tsx, components/layout/{page-shell,page-header}.tsx. Files (rewritten): components/ItemCard.tsx (unifies 3 prior implementations), components/ui/skeleton.tsx. Files (modified): app/layout.tsx (removed nested <main>), app/globals.css (glass declaration-order fix). Installed: class-variance-authority.
Decisions:
- Button: rewrote with cva, settled the rounded-xl-vs-rounded-full conflict by making rounded-full the only radius (matches what's already shipping — the hero pill buttons). Fixed the asChild-loses-motion bug properly: motion.create(Slot) and motion.create("button") both get the identical whileHover/whileTap spring, instead of the old motion.div wrapper that silently only wrapped the non-asChild path (every <Link>-as-button in the app was losing its hover animation). Hit and fixed a real TS error doing this: framer-motion's onDrag/onAnimationStart/etc conflict in type with the native HTML handlers of the same name — Omit the native ones from ButtonProps
- PageShell now owns the single <main> per page; removed layout.tsx's wrapping <main> (every page already renders its own, so this was a nested-landmark bug on every single page, not just a hypothetical one — confirmed via grep that all page.tsx files already have their own <main>)
- ItemCard unification: closet's grid tile was left OUT of the unified component on purpose — it renders ClosetItem (category/color, no price/likes/seller), a genuinely different data shape from marketplace Item/WishlistItem, and forcing it into one interface would mean an all-optional prop soup. Feed and Wishlist both got folded in via an onRemove prop (wishlist) vs internal optimistic like state (feed, only active when is_liked/likes_count are present) — one component, one canonical 4:5 aspect ratio (was 4:5 feed / 1:1 wishlist), one visual language. Made the price/title scrim ALWAYS visible rather than hover-only (the old behavior hid the price behind a hover state that doesn't work on touch devices — a real UX bug, not just a visual one) and reserved the actual glass-dark blur treatment for the single corner action button, which is the one part of the card that's legitimately "glass over photo"
- Skeleton's default changed bg-white/10 → bg-base-2 (was invisible on light; 3 of 4 callers were already overriding it, drops/page.tsx wasn't and rendered nothing)
- REAL BUG caught via compiled-CSS inspection, not source reading: Lightning CSS (Tailwind v4's processor) was silently dropping the unprefixed `backdrop-filter` declaration when it appeared BEFORE `-webkit-backdrop-filter` in the same rule, leaving glass-dark/glass-light with zero actual blur despite background/border applying correctly. Verified by fetching the actual served chunk and grepping it — the source had both lines, the compiled output only had -webkit-. Fixed by reordering (webkit first, standard second); re-verified via getComputedStyle that backdrop-filter: blur(12px) now applies. This would have shipped as "glass that doesn't blur" across every Phase 3 use of the surgical-glass rule if not caught here
- Verification note: the browser tool's screenshot capture returned blank at any non-zero scroll position all session (confirmed independent of my code via elementFromPoint + computed-style ground-truth checks, which showed correct rendering at the exact pixel coordinates) — treated as a tooling limitation, not a rendering bug, and worked around with DOM/network/computed-style checks instead of screenshots for this phase

[2026-08-27] Production Hardening P1 — stop the bleeding (reliability + payments) (NOT pushed/deployed — awaiting approval)
Files: backend/Dockerfile, backend/config/settings.py, backend/core/models.py, backend/core/views.py, backend/core/serializers.py, backend/notifications/signals.py, backend/core/migrations/0012_processedstripeevent_and_more.py (new), backend/core/tests/{test_orders.py,test_notifications.py}
Decisions:
- Full Phase 0 audit run and independently verified (grep/read against source, gcloud describe against the live service, curl against live endpoints) before any code changed — not taken on faith from a subagent summary. Ranked findings and a 7-phase execution plan approved via ExitPlanMode before this work started; this entry covers P1 only (Critical items C1/C2/C3, High items H2/H8)
- C1 — Dockerfile CMD changed from `migrate --noinput && exec gunicorn` to `migrate --noinput || echo ...; exec gunicorn` (`;` not `&&`). The `&&` chaining is what took down every endpoint, including /api/health/, during today's real production incident: Supabase free-tier auto-paused, migrate couldn't reach the DB, and the whole container died instead of just the DB-dependent paths. Migration now retries on every future cold start regardless
- C2 — added `@throttle_classes([])` to stripe_webhook. Verified directly that `@permission_classes([AllowAny])` does NOT disable DRF's DEFAULT_THROTTLE_CLASSES — the webhook was landing in the 100/hour anon bucket, so a busy day or a Stripe retry storm would get 429'd, which Stripe logs as a delivery failure and keeps retrying without the order ever fulfilling
- C3 — handle_checkout_completion rewritten: transaction.atomic() + select_for_update() row-locks the matching PENDING orders, and a new ProcessedStripeEvent model (event_id unique, DB-enforced) rejects a redelivered event.id before it can run twice. Verified there was previously zero transaction.atomic/select_for_update anywhere in the entire backend — the existing test only proved sequential replay was safe, not concurrent. Also added a payment_status == 'paid' check before fulfilling (checkout.session.completed fires even for sessions that didn't collect payment)
- H2 — Order.stripe_payment_intent renamed to stripe_checkout_session_id (it never held a PaymentIntent id, only the cs_... Checkout Session id) and indexed; added a real stripe_payment_intent_id (pi_...) field, captured from the webhook payload on fulfillment, so refunds/disputes have an actual join key against Stripe's dashboard
- H8 — the two order-paid emails (buyer confirmation, seller notification) now fire via transaction.on_commit instead of synchronously inside the save() that's called while holding the new select_for_update() locks — a slow mail server no longer extends lock hold time or risks exceeding Stripe's webhook response timeout (which would trigger the very retries C3 guards against). Added EMAIL_TIMEOUT=10 (smtplib has no timeout by default) as defense-in-depth
- Caught a would-be destructive migration before it touched anything: the autodetector's first pass produced RemoveField+AddField for the stripe_payment_intent rename, which would have DROPPED the column and every real production order's Stripe session id. Hand-edited to migrations.RenameField (metadata-only) + AlterField. Verified two ways: makemigrations --check --dry-run reports no drift against the hand-edit, and sqlmigrate confirms the actual SQL is a plain ALTER TABLE...RENAME COLUMN + two non-blocking CREATE INDEX + a nullable ADD COLUMN, all in one transaction — no table rewrite, no data loss
- Also fixed while already rewriting create_checkout_session for C2/H2: it was echoing str(e) from any Stripe exception straight to the client, which can leak internal detail (request ids, key-related hints). Now logs the real exception server-side and returns a generic message. Small, in-scope, called out explicitly rather than folded in silently
