Decisions:
- REAL USER-FACING BUG found and fixed, discovered by actually running the app rather than reading code: a logged-out visitor on the PUBLIC homepage was force-redirected to /login roughly 30 seconds after arriving. Cause: NotificationBell polls /api/notifications/ every 30s regardless of auth state, that 401s for an anonymous visitor, and the axios interceptor treated ANY 401 as "session expired" → window.location.href = '/login'. Reproduced cleanly in the browser twice (land on /, wait, observe navigation to /login and the JS context being wiped), and confirmed PRE-EXISTING via git show 5fd6422 — the same unconditional redirect predates all of this hardening work, so it is not a P2 regression. This made the public marketplace effectively unbrowsable for anonymous visitors, the exact audience a public marketplace needs
- Fixed at both layers rather than just the nearest one: (1) logoutLocally() in lib/api.ts now only redirects if the visitor actually HAD a session (an access or refresh token present) — someone never logged in isn't "logged out" and has nothing to re-authenticate; (2) NotificationBell no longer polls at all when signed out, so the guaranteed-401 request isn't made in the first place. Verified the fix with the identical reproduction: still on / after 43s, JS context intact, zero notification polls
- Polling backoff (the plan's P5 item 23): both loops already had unmount cleanup and document.hidden pausing from an earlier session; the genuine gap was no backoff on failure — during a backend outage every open tab kept hammering a dead server at full rate (messages polls every 5s), which is the shape of load that extends an outage rather than riding it out. New usePolling hook: self-scheduling setTimeout (not setInterval, whose period is fixed at creation and can't widen), exponential backoff doubling per consecutive failure up to a 5-minute cap, reset on first success, callback held in a ref so inline arrow functions don't restart the loop every render. Also means the next poll is only scheduled after the previous settles, so slow responses can't stack overlapping in-flight requests
- Deliberately stopped swallowing errors inside the polled callbacks — the previous inline versions caught-and-ignored, which would make every poll look successful and defeat backoff entirely. User-visible behavior is unchanged (state is simply left as-is on failure)
- New ErrorState primitive + wired into 8 pages (wishlist, orders, closet, drops, leaderboard, messages, profile, and the items grid). The audit that motivated this: EVERY data-fetching page console.error'd a failed fetch and then fell through to its EmptyState, so a backend outage rendered as "You have no orders yet" / "Your wishlist is empty" / (worst) "User not found" on a real person's profile. That's not cosmetic — it actively misinforms (a seller concludes their listings vanished) and hides real outages from the people best placed to report them. This project has already had one total backend outage during which the app would have looked serenely, incorrectly empty. Distinct from the existing Alert primitive, which is an inline banner for form/action errors
- profile/[username] specifically now distinguishes a genuine 404 (EmptyState: "No account exists with @username") from any other failure (ErrorState) — previously all failures rendered "User not found"
- Verified ErrorState renders correctly in the browser against the real dev server (screenshot + page text) rather than assuming from the code
- Keyboard focus audit: first measurement (programmatic el.focus() + computed style) suggested 21 of 28 interactive elements had no focus indicator — that was a MEASUREMENT ARTIFACT, since .focus() doesn't trigger :focus-visible. Re-tested with real Tab keypresses, which showed the browser default outline applying correctly. The genuine finding was narrower and real: exactly 2 elements killed the outline with focus:outline-none and nothing replacing it — UserDropdown's account button (completely invisible to keyboard users) and SearchAutocomplete's input (border-color change only, far weaker than a ring). Both now carry an explicit focus-visible ring. Zero unreplaced outline-removals remain repo-wide
- Introduced and then fixed my own eslint error mid-phase: the signed-in check was first written as setState-in-effect (the repo's existing anti-pattern), replaced with useSyncExternalStore + an explicit server snapshot, which is the correct tool for reading localStorage under SSR and avoids an extra render pass
