Decisions:
- New identity "After Hours" — dark gallery canvas + fashion-editorial typography, replacing Paper Editorial wholesale. Direction chosen by the user from four options after research into threejs.org (which is itself flat/minimal — its power is a dense thumbnail wall, not a 3D hero), Awwwards e-commerce winners, and 2026 editorial/kinetic-type trends
- Inverted the ramp VALUES in globals.css rather than renaming tokens. The legacy Solarized names are positional but were used semantically consistently (dark end for text, light end for surfaces), so flipping the values re-themed all 181 legacy call sites in one move with every usage still landing correctly. The --ink/--paper aliases survive because they were named for ROLE not value: on black, ink is light and paper is the dark ground — the metaphor still holds
- Palette computed, not eyeballed, and CORRECTED MID-FLIGHT by a browser audit. My first ramp had --muted at #6e6e70, which I documented as "AA-large only" — then the app uses it for small body copy in 37 places. A programmatic contrast sweep across every rendered route caught it at 3.15:1 on raised surfaces (the binding constraint, since surface is lighter than canvas and therefore the harder test — not the canvas, which is what I'd checked). Re-solved the whole mid-range to pass AA on BOTH grounds while keeping the ramp strictly monotonic; verified descending luminance so the positional names keep meaning something
- Accent is acid citron #d4ff4f (17.17:1) — deliberately the one loud note, chosen so the palette reads thrift/flyer rather than generic luxury-minimal dark
- Elevation reworked for dark: the five black-based shadow tokens did nothing on a near-black canvas. Elevation is now surface-lightness + hairline, with much deeper shadows retained only for genuinely floating things. The glass rule INVERTED too — dark glass is invisible against a dark ground, so light glass is now the one that reads over imagery
- Typography: Fraunces -> Bodoni Moda (true Didone, the Vogue register), Geist -> Archivo (grotesk; picked over a hairline serif specifically because light-on-dark type gains optical weight and thin strokes break up). Hard rule encoded in the .font-display comment: Bodoni is display-only, never below ~32px. Verified via getComputedStyle that both fonts actually resolve rather than silently falling back to Preflight's stack — the exact bug class that shipped here once before
- Audited every light-canvas assumption the inversion breaks. Found and fixed 19 real breakages across 12 files: text-white sitting on fills that inverted to light (bg-ink/bg-base-03/bg-primary/bg-error). A first pass with a line-scoped regex fixed 15 but MISSED three that a line-based match can't see — CountdownTimer (bg-ink and text-white on different JSX lines), ConversationList's unread badge, and the Button danger variant, where white on the new light --error measures 3.03:1. Also caught MessageInput's literal bg-white textarea whose text token had inverted to light (invisible), and two spinners using border-white inside now-light buttons. Deliberately left the 14 text-white usages that sit over photography or glass scrims — those are still correct
- Deleted RippleText and replaced /login's two headlines with real Bodoni type. This fixed a PRE-EXISTING bug I found while auditing, not one I introduced (verified via git diff against main that I had never touched the file): the component sizes its canvas backing store from canvas.offsetWidth, which measures 0 for a w-full canvas at effect time, so both headlines on /login rendered as blank canvases — confirmed live at backing width 0 / CSS width 505. It also ran an rAF loop that never idled with no prefers-reduced-motion guard, on the first page many visitors see. The redesign's identity IS display type, so replacing the canvas with real type fixed the bug and served the design at once
- New src/lib/cloudinary.ts. Measured, not estimated: a real production listing's 982 KB PNG becomes 41 KB through c_fill,ar,g_auto,q_auto,f_auto,w_800 — a 24x reduction on the heaviest asset class in the app, free, no infra change. Equally important for the design: g_auto subject-aware cropping forces one shared frame across chaotic user uploads (production's single real photo is literally a screenshot), which is what lets a grid read as a gallery at all. Verified every generated URL resolves 200 against live Cloudinary, and that re-wrapping an already-transformed URL replaces rather than stacks the transform segment
- Added isCloudinary() + unoptimized on next/image for Cloudinary sources. Caught by reading the actual network request rather than assuming: a Cloudinary-transformed URL was still being passed through /_next/image, so the image was optimized TWICE — two lossy passes, and on Vercel each one is a billed image optimization, which matters on a $0-infra project. Verified in the DOM that the real photo now goes direct to Cloudinary with its transform while local placeholders still correctly use next/image
- Verification: programmatic contrast audit (not judgment) run across every route via same-origin iframes. 9 of 11 routes genuinely rendered and came back clean. Being explicit rather than claiming full coverage: /dashboard and /messages could NOT be audited — they redirect to /login when logged out, and seeding a fake token didn't help because their API call 401s and the P5 interceptor redirects. Their contrast remains unverified and should be checked in R3 with real credentials
- Gates: tsc clean; eslint exactly 18 errors = the pre-existing baseline, zero new; backend 70/70 green. The network-request buffer went stale mid-session (a known limitation in this environment, already recorded in an earlier entry) so image verification was done against the DOM, which is ground truth

[2026-08-28] "After Hours" redesign R2 — primitives: square radii, Badge/Avatar/Dialog/Tabs/Separator (NOT pushed — awaiting review)
Files: frontend/src/components/ui/{button,card,input,textarea,select,alert,skeleton,empty-state,error-state}.tsx (restyled), frontend/src/components/ui/{badge,avatar,dialog,separator,tabs}.tsx (new), frontend/src/app/orders/page.tsx (adopts Tabs), frontend/src/components/SearchBar.tsx (adopts Dialog), frontend/src/components/ConversationList.tsx (adopts Avatar+Badge), + 28 files swept from panel radii (2xl/3xl/xl/lg/md) to rounded-none
Decisions:
- Settled the radius system: 133 usages were spread across 6 scales despite existing comments in button.tsx/card.tsx claiming "one canonical radius" (a claim the code never actually enforced). Editorial wants edges — every PANEL radius (rounded-2xl/3xl/xl/lg/md, 66 usages across 28 files) swept to rounded-none via a script, verified with correct \b word boundaries after a first attempt (bare sed, no \b support on macOS) silently changed nothing. rounded-full (56 usages) was NOT blanket-swept — classified each usage as a genuine circle (h-N w-N box, or equal icon-button padding) vs a text-bearing pill, and squared only the 25 pills, keeping the 31 real circles (avatars, spinners, icon buttons)
- Button rebuilt for the register: rounded-none, mono uppercase wide-tracked labels (the museum-wall-label voice), citron reserved as `secondary` so it stays rare and keeps its punch. Toned the hover/tap spring down (was scale 1.03 — visibly softens a square corner mid-animation) to a 1px lift with no scale, since colour now carries the hover state
- Five missing primitives added, each because it was hand-rolled inconsistently at 3+ call sites: Badge (mono/uppercase, matches Button's voice), Avatar (deliberately stayed circular — the one shape a face suits — with a Cloudinary face-aware crop and an initial-letter fallback most hand-rolled versions lacked), Separator (plain hairline + a labelled "section break" form), Tabs (generalized from /orders' hand-rolled version — fixed a latent bug where the old hardcoded layoutId="activeTab" would collide if two tab bars were ever mounted at once, made it a required unique-by-default prop), Dialog
- Dialog is where real bugs were caught by testing in the browser rather than trusting the implementation, in order:
  1. Focus never moved into the dialog on open. Traced to onClose being a fresh function identity every render (near-always an inline arrow at the call site) sitting in the effect's dependency array — the effect tore down and re-ran on every re-render, and since teardown restores focus to the trigger, focus was yanked back out immediately. Fixed by holding onClose in a ref so the effect depends only on `open`
  2. Even after that fix, focus still didn't move — traced by instrumenting the component directly (not guessing) to requestAnimationFrame never firing in this Browser pane's current state within a 30s timeout. This is a real, already-documented limitation of this environment (P5 hit the same document.visibilityState issue), but it also indicts the DESIGN: a dialog whose focus-handling depends on rAF polling can fail identically in a real backgrounded/throttled tab, which is exactly the case this code exists to handle correctly. Replaced the useEffect+rAF-poll with useLayoutEffect + a single synchronous focus call — no polling needed, since children are already-rendered JSX by the time the effect runs, not something that mounts later
  3. AnimatePresence was leaving an orphaned, un-rerendered subtree in the DOM after the exit animation instead of unmounting it — caught by reading computed opacity (0) and hit-testing rather than trusting that the dialog "looked closed". The leftover node is a `fixed inset-0` full-viewport backdrop with pointer-events:auto, which would silently swallow every click on the page after one open/close cycle. Adding a React key and forcing pointer-events on exit both failed to reach it, because the orphaned subtree was no longer under React's control at all. Removed AnimatePresence entirely — unmounting is now plain conditional rendering (`if (!open) return null`), which cannot fail this way; the exit animation was judged not worth this risk on a real store. Verified across two full open/close cycles via both the Escape and backdrop-click paths, with an explicit assertion that zero backdrop nodes remain
- SearchBar adopted Dialog, replacing a hand-rolled overlay with no escape handling, no scroll lock, no focus management, and no dialog semantics
- Adopted the new primitives at real call sites rather than leaving them unused: ConversationList's hand-rolled circular-avatar-with-generic-icon-fallback became Avatar (which also gets a real initial-letter fallback and Cloudinary face-aware cropping, neither of which the old code had), and its hand-rolled unread-count span became Badge
- CartDrawer deliberately NOT converted to Dialog — it's a slide-in side sheet, a genuinely different interaction pattern from Dialog's centered/sheet-from-top layout, and forcing the mismatch would make it worse. Left for page-level work in R3
- Gates: tsc clean; eslint exactly 18 errors = the pre-existing baseline (checked twice, once briefly regressed to 20 from two orphaned imports left behind when Tabs/Button replaced hand-rolled JSX, both removed); backend 70/70 untouched
